---
/**
 * The rank is not a metaphor: it is the number of singular values that are not zero.
 *
 * "The correction has to pass through eight dimensions" is easy to write and easy to believe
 * without checking. The check is one line of linear algebra, and it is what this figure draws:
 * the singular values of `W` and of `ΔW = (alpha/r)·B·A` for the same matrix, side by side.
 * `W` has 768 of them and every one is non-zero; `ΔW` has exactly **8**, and the ninth is not
 * small, it is zero.
 *
 * Measured, not drawn: `labs/m4/lora_spectrum.py` on the published `lora-e4` over the query slice
 * of `blocks.0.attn.qkv`, written to `src/data/lora-spectrum.json`. Each row is scaled by its own
 * largest value, and the figure says so, because what it compares is the **shape** of the two
 * spectra and `ΔW` is a twenty-fifth of `W` in norm -- drawn on one scale the bottom row would be
 * invisible and the point would be lost.
 *
 * The marker walks the positions and the bottom row stops answering at the ninth. That is the
 * only motion and it is the whole figure. The bar grid is 26.6 units, which is the step of
 * `fig-spectrum-sweep` in `global.css` (532 units in twenty steps), and starts one step in, so the
 * sweep lands on a bar at every step and the reduced-motion parking (`translateX(298px)`) lands on
 * the ninth bar, the first zero.
 */
import spectrum from '../../data/lora-spectrum.json';

const KEEP = 20;
const base = spectrum.base.slice(0, KEEP);
const delta = spectrum.delta.slice(0, KEEP);
const step = 26.6;
const x = (index: number) => 86.6 + step * index;
const bar = 18;
const height = (value: number, all: number[], top: number) =>
  Math.max(0.6, (value / Math.max(...all)) * top);
const percent = (value: number) => `${(value * 100).toFixed(1).replace('.', ',')} %`;
---

<figure class="fig fig--spectrum" tabindex="0">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 640 232"
    role="img"
    aria-label={`Valores singulares de la matriz de consulta del primer bloque y de su corrección de LoRA, cada fila a su propia escala. La matriz tiene 768 y ninguno es cero. La corrección tiene exactamente ${spectrum.nonzero_delta}: del noveno en adelante son cero, no pequeños. En norma, la corrección es el ${percent(spectrum.relative_norm)} de la matriz.`}
  >
    <g font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--muted)">
      <text x="10" y="18" fill="var(--ink)">
        <tspan font-size="10">W</tspan>
        <tspan dx="6">768 × 768 · 768 valores singulares, ninguno cero</tspan>
      </text>
      <text x="614" y="18" text-anchor="end" font-size="9">
        (cada fila a su escala)
      </text>
      {base.map((value, index) => (
        <rect
          x={x(index) - bar / 2}
          y={78 - height(value, base, 50)}
          width={bar}
          height={height(value, base, 50)}
          fill="var(--diagram-stroke)"
          fill-opacity="0.45"
          stroke="var(--line)"
          stroke-width="0.6"
        />
      ))}
      <line x1="40" y1="78" x2="614" y2="78" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="0.8"></line>

      <text x="10" y="112" fill="var(--ink)">
        <tspan>ΔW = (α/r)·B·A</tspan>
        <tspan dx="6">
          · {spectrum.nonzero_delta} valores singulares · {percent(spectrum.relative_norm)} de la
          norma de W
        </tspan>
      </text>
      {delta.map((value, index) => (
        <rect
          x={x(index) - bar / 2}
          y={172 - height(value, delta, 50)}
          width={bar}
          height={height(value, delta, 50)}
          fill={index < spectrum.r ? 'var(--accent)' : 'none'}
          fill-opacity={index < spectrum.r ? 0.55 : 0}
          stroke={index < spectrum.r ? 'var(--accent)' : 'var(--line)'}
          stroke-width="0.6"
          stroke-dasharray={index < spectrum.r ? undefined : '2 2'}
        />
      ))}
      <line x1="40" y1="172" x2="614" y2="172" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="0.8"></line>

      <line
        x1={x(spectrum.r) - bar / 2 - 3}
        y1="122"
        x2={x(spectrum.r) - bar / 2 - 3}
        y2="182"
        stroke="var(--accent)"
        stroke-width="1.2"
        stroke-dasharray="3 3"
      ></line>
      <text x={x(spectrum.r) + 4} y="196" font-size="9" fill="var(--accent-text-aa)">
        a partir de aquí son cero, no pequeños
      </text>

      {[0, 4, 9, 14, 19].map((index) => (
        <text x={x(index)} y="214" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">
          {index + 1}
        </text>
      ))}
      <text x="10" y="214" font-size="9">
        índice
      </text>

      <g class="fig-spectrum__marker">
        <rect
          x={-bar / 2 - 3}
          y="24"
          width={bar + 6}
          height="152"
          fill="var(--accent)"
          fill-opacity="0.1"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="0.8"
        ></rect>
      </g>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="caption">
    El rango no es una metáfora: son ocho direcciones, y la novena no es pequeña, es cero. Cada fila
    va a su propia escala, porque en norma la corrección es una vigésima quinta parte de la matriz y
    a una sola escala no se vería. Medido sobre el adaptador publicado con{' '}
    <code>labs/m4/lora_spectrum.py</code>.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
